1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:15,080
Episode One ended on a confusion almost
every beginner hits: someone says "install
Java," and three acronyms appear — JDK,
JRE, JVM — as if they were the same thing.
That confusion is not vocabulary trivia.

2
00:00:15,199 --> 00:00:25,519
We already know source becomes bytecode,
and a JVM runs that bytecode. So if the
JVM is the engine, what did you actually
install when you typed a command and
waited for a download?

3
00:00:25,699 --> 00:00:35,619
Start from the moment you try to compile
HelloWorld. Look at this bash for a
moment. If javac is missing, the first
command fails.

4
00:00:35,739 --> 00:00:45,979
You can have a machine that runs Java
applications and still be unable to
compile anything. That failure splits the
world into two jobs: building programs,
and running them.

5
00:00:46,099 --> 00:00:59,579
The toolkit that contains the compiler —
and the other developer tools around it —
is the JDK, the Java Development Kit.
javac lives there. So do tools such as
jar, jlink, and diagnostics you will meet
later.

6
00:00:59,699 --> 00:01:09,259
When a tutorial says "install the JDK," it
means: give yourself the toolbox needed to
create Java software. Compiling is only
half the path.

7
00:01:09,379 --> 00:01:23,140
After javac produces HelloWorld.class, the
second command starts a process that can
load bytecode and execute it. That process
is centered on the JVM — commonly HotSpot.
The JVM is the engine.

8
00:01:23,259 --> 00:01:31,699
It does not care that you wrote the source
this morning. It cares that valid bytecode
arrived and needs to run. So where does
the JRE fit?

9
00:01:31,879 --> 00:01:42,359
Historically, the JRE — Java Runtime
Environment — named the runtime idea: the
libraries and launcher you need to run an
application without shipping the full
developer toolbox.

10
00:01:42,479 --> 00:01:48,559
In older distributions, you could install
a JRE on a server that should only run
apps, not compile them.

11
00:01:48,679 --> 00:01:56,679
In modern JDK distributions the lines are
blurrier, because a JDK download usually
includes what you need to run as well as
compile.

12
00:01:56,799 --> 00:02:10,759
The useful distinction remains:
development tools versus the runtime that
executes bytecode. Hold the layers
lightly: JDK is the toolbox, the runtime
is where the program lives, the JVM is the
engine inside.

13
00:02:10,879 --> 00:02:23,719
You write HelloWorld.java. The JDK's javac
turns it into bytecode. You run java
HelloWorld. That launcher starts a JVM,
the JVM loads the class, finds main, and
executes the print.

14
00:02:23,840 --> 00:02:36,560
Same story as Episode One — now with
clearer labels. Once the happy path is
clear, production life introduces sharper
mistakes. First: calling everything "the
JDK," including the running process.

15
00:02:36,680 --> 00:02:49,520
A production container may be executing a
JVM with your application. That is not
"the JDK running." The JDK was involved
when the code was built. The running
process is a JVM executing bytecode.

16
00:02:49,640 --> 00:02:56,520
Mixing those words at 2 a.m. makes it
harder to know whether you are debugging a
build problem or a runtime problem.

17
00:02:56,640 --> 00:03:09,160
Second: compiling with one Java version in
CI and running a different major version
in production without checking
compatibility. A build that succeeds on
JDK 21 can still surprise a Java 17
runtime.

18
00:03:09,280 --> 00:03:21,159
The mismatch lives exactly where we just
named the layers: the toolkit that
compiled versus the engine that later
tries to run. So Episode One asked why
Java exists and showed bytecode plus the
JVM.

19
00:03:21,280 --> 00:03:32,280
Today we answered the install confusion:
JDK for building, runtime for running, JVM
as the engine. The commands javac and java
are no longer mysterious synonyms.

20
00:03:32,400 --> 00:03:43,040
And yet a new frustration appears as soon
as those tools work. You write a class,
name a file, maybe add a package, and the
compiler or launcher refuses until the
pieces agree.

21
00:03:43,159 --> 00:03:49,359
Why does Java care so much about where
main lives, what the filename is, and how
packages map to folders?

22
00:03:49,539 --> 00:04:00,979
That structural stubbornness is how the
compiler and JVM find your code — which is
why the next natural question is: why do
filenames, packages, and main have to
agree before anything will run?
